On 6/5/2012 2:36 PM, Winston Chang wrote:

> On Tue, Jun 5, 2012 at 2:54 PM, Winston Chang<winstonchang1-Re5J...@public.gmane.org>wrote:
>
>> Hi all -
>>
>> Some updates on the theme system
>>
>> I've implemented the + operator for themes:
>> https://github.com/hadley/ggplot2/pull/573/files#diff-9
>>
>> Now you can do things like:
>>    theme_bw() + opts(axis.title.x = element_text(colour='red'))
>>
>> I've also updated theme_bw() to use it:
>> https://github.com/hadley/ggplot2/pull/573/files#diff-12
>>
>>
>> The next steps are to deal with theme_update and update_element. I've
>> always found it kind of confusing to work with them. Here are some changes
>> that I think would be beneficial:
>>
>> * Get rid of theme_update and update_element.
>> * Add an operator like %|%, which can be used to update individual
>> properties of elements. For example:
>>    mytheme<- theme_bw()
>>    mytheme<- mytheme | opts(text = element_text(colour="red"))
>>
>> One consequence of removing update_theme and update_element is that the
>> current theme can't be modified in place. You'd have to do something like
>> the above, and then:
>>    theme_set(mytheme)
>>
>> Thoughts? I'm not sure if these changes should go in 0.9.2 because they'll
>> break some functionality. But I suspect that people don't use theme_update
>> and update_element very often anyway.
>>
>>
> I went ahead and implemented a | operator for modifying element properties.
> Now you can do this to modify specified properties of theme elements:
>    mytheme<- theme_bw() | opts(text=element_text(colour='red'))
>    qplot(1:3, 1:3) + mytheme
>
> Notice that this is different from using +, which replaces the whole
> element. For example, this won't work because text properties like size,
> family, etc, are NULL:
>    mytheme<- theme_bw() + opts(text=element_text(colour='red'))
>    qplot(1:3, 1:3) + mytheme

I must be missing something. Why should `+` replace the whole element?
If you had

   + opts(axis.title.x = theme_text(colour='red'))

then it should replace everything, because that was the behavior of
theme_text; but if you have

   + opts(axis.text.x = element_text(colour='red'))

then it should just replace that one part, yes?

Or is the issue that in your example you are setting `text`, which being
a root node, must have all the properties defined?

I'm not seeing the distinction between what `+` is supposed to do with
an `opts` versus what `|` is.

Oh, I think I might have it. And it was something I misunderstood about
how the theme system is going to work.  The way it is written now, if
you assign something to an element, that replaces the entire element at
that level, but anything not defined at that level will still be
inherited from higher up the hierarchy. But something that was defined
at that level would be lost.  Let's see if I can put together an example:

Let's say the theme consists of (using opts notation, since that is how
I am thinking about it):

opts(text = element_text(... something appropriate ...),
      axis.text = element_text(size = rel(0.8), angle=45),
      axis.text.x = element_text(colour="green"),
      axis.text.y = element_text(colour="red", angle=90))

Now say that I really want the y axis text to be purple instead of red
(I never said I had good taste in colors), so I would add:

   + opts(axis.text.y = element_text(colour="purple"))

This would reset the angle, then, to whatever it would inherit from
further up the hierarchy (45)? Or it would stay at 90?

I think it should stay at 90 since it has not been explicitly overridden
at that level of the hierarchy.

> So far, so good. But the syntax for adding to a ggplot object (not yet
