That sounds like a good idea.

Right now, if you define mytheme as above (with some null properties for 'text'), and then add it to a plot like this:

   qplot(1:3, 1:3) + mytheme

it ends up inheriting the missing properties from the current default theme, like theme_grey. This is because it's added to the ggplot object with the + operator (not %+replace%), so the non-null properties from mytheme$text update the default theme's $text properties, but mytheme$text doesn't replace the default theme's $text element entirely.

If someone wrote a theme with null properties for a root element, they would never see any errors when making a plot, because the null properties would just get inherited from the default theme. So I think you're right that it should be checked at the call to  %+replace%.

> What about the distinction between NULL and inherit() that Hadley was mentionning? I must admit I am not sure I understand the difference and the merit of having a difference but that seemed important ;)

It wasn't 100% clear to me either, but it turned out that this wouldn't work... the updating vs. replacing behavior had to be defined in the operator, and not the element itself.

-Winston

