>> instruction available, just inherit".
>
> You need to distinguish between
>
> element_text(colour = NULL)
>
> and
>
> element_text()
>
> which both produce the same output - i.e. you need to distinguish
> between saying that the element should inherit from its parent, vs
> stay unchanged.  I think the clearest behaviour (in line with how
> scales work) is for:
>
> + element_text(colour = NULL)
>
> to leave the colour unchanged and
>
> + element_text(colour = inherit())
>
> to force colour to be inherited from the parent.

This took me a few readings before I understood the significance of what
you are saying.  In case anyone else is as slow as me, I'll give an
example. Starting with a theme

theme_gray() + opts(axis.text = element_text(size = rel(0.8), angle=45),

                     axis.text.x = element_text(colour="green"),
                     axis.text.y = element_text(colour="red", angle=90))

there is needs to be a distinction when modifying (say) axis.text.y as
to whether the angle is to be unchanged from its current value (90), or
whether the angle is no longer specified at this level of the hierarchy
and thus should inherit (45). Setting it to some other specific value
(say -90) is straightforward.  The relevance of this distinction is when
a value has been specified at that specific level of the hierarchy, but
the change is to make it inherit instead.

> One other thing we'd need to make clear is that
>
> + element_text(size = rel(0.8)) + element_text(size = rel(0.5))
>
> gives a text size of 0.5 relative to it's parent, not 0.8 * 0.5 =
> 0.4.

I agree that is how it should be, and it is consistent with the
principle that subsequent assignments to the same part of the same
element replace the previous value. However, with rel(), it would be
good to make this clear.

> Hadley

