>> that's the case, maybe the %+replace% (why not just %+% as in ggplot when
>> you want to replace the data) could emit a warning/error when this occurs -
>> that's if you want to be reaaally thorough of course.
>>
>
> That sounds like a good idea.
>
> Right now, if you define mytheme as above (with some null properties for
> 'text'), and then add it to a plot like this:
>     qplot(1:3, 1:3) + mytheme
> it ends up inheriting the missing properties from the current default
> theme, like theme_grey. This is because it's added to the ggplot object
> with the + operator (not %+replace%), so the non-null properties from
> mytheme$text update the default theme's $text properties, but mytheme$text
> doesn't replace the default theme's $text element entirely.
>
> If someone wrote a theme with null properties for a root element, they
> would never see any errors when making a plot, because the null properties
> would just get inherited from the default theme. So I think you're right
> that it should be checked at the call to  %+replace%.

That seems like a nice side effect. Any theme is defined relative to the
default theme for anything that that theme does not define itself.

